LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — Reviews on structure and evidence", "12", "<p>Modules 1-5 governed pay as it IS; this module governs how it CHANGES — the review cycle that moves the fixed pay itself — and its thesis is the sixth law forming: <b>pay reviews run on structure and evidence, not on squeak and mood.</b></p><p><b>Why pay must be reviewed at all.</b> The Nigerian reality stated first, because it drives everything: unreviewed nominal pay DECAYS — inflation erodes real pay silently, month by month, so the firm that 'kept salaries stable' for three years has in truth cut everyone's real pay substantially without a single decision (the decay is a pay cut nobody announced, and the workforce experiences it exactly as one: the HR track's attrition referendum reads it back as the year-two exodus, the side-hustle drift, and the counter-offer season) — which converts the review from a generosity question into a MAINTENANCE question: the review cycle is how a firm keeps the pay bargain's real value deliberate instead of letting arithmetic quietly break it. The second driver beside it: EVIDENCE accumulates — the HR track's performance machinery produces calibrated, documented ratings annually, and evidence that changes nothing teaches everyone that the folder was theatre (HR 5's ambush law has a payroll corollary: the appraisal that never touches pay eventually never touches effort).</p><p><b>The two inputs.</b> Every review cycle reconciles exactly two forces, and naming them keeps the cycle honest: the FIRM'S CAPACITY (what the budget can carry — Chapter 6's envelope, set from the numbers before the cycle opens, because the affordability decided after seeing the wish-list is a negotiation, while the envelope decided before is a constraint everyone shares) and the PERSON'S EVIDENCE (the calibrated rating, the band position, the certified growth — the HR track's outputs, consumed as the cycle's raw material). The cycle's machinery — the matrix (Chapter 3), the bands (Chapter 4), the governance (Chapter 7) — exists to reconcile these two inputs EVENLY: same evidence, same treatment, inside the same envelope.</p><p><b>One cycle, not rolling favours.</b> The structural decision that shapes everything: pay changes happen at the CYCLE (the annual review, on the calendar, all at once) plus the defined movement events (the promotion, the confirmation step where policy grants one — HR 7's documents), and NOT as a rolling stream of individual renegotiations — because the rolling model is the favour system with a calendar problem (the raise granted to whoever asked loudest, whenever they asked — module 1's negotiated-from-memory disease applied to changes; the quiet raise that module 7's governance will formally outlaw), while the cycle model concentrates the firm's one hardest conversation into one season with one process, one envelope, and one announcement — which is cheaper in management attention, fairer by construction, and defensible forever. The module's map follows the cycle's own arc: the calendar event (2), the matrix (3), the bands beneath (4), the promotions and equity reads (5), the budget above (6), the governance and letters around (7) — and HealthTrade's first cycle run end to end (8).</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The review cycle: the calendar event", "13", "<p>The cycle as machinery: its stages, in order, each feeding the next — the run's ritual discipline applied to the year's biggest pay event. The cycle chapter.</p><p><b>The stages.</b> The sequence, walked: <b>1 — the envelope set</b> (Chapter 6's budget work concluding BEFORE the cycle opens: the total increment pool as a number the owner has already accepted — the cycle that opens without an envelope closes as an argument); <b>2 — the evidence in</b> (the HR track's appraisal season concluding first by design — the calibrated ratings landing as the cycle's input file: the two calendars sequenced deliberately, appraisals then reviews, because ratings finalised DURING pay season are ratings written backwards from desired raises — the calibration integrity of HR 5 protected by the calendar itself); <b>3 — the matrix applied</b> (Chapter 3 — the ratings and band positions converted to proposed increments, mechanically, as the STARTING grid); <b>4 — the adjustments within governance</b> (the below-band priorities, the retention flags, the equity corrections — Chapter 5's reads applied as deliberate exceptions with written reasons, inside the envelope: the matrix proposes, humans decide, the envelope constrains); <b>5 — the approval</b> (the full grid — every name, current, proposed, new band position, cost — approved by the authority in one sitting: the review's version of module 3's names-reading approval); <b>6 — the letters</b> (Chapter 7 — individual, with effective dates and net effects); <b>7 — the execution</b> (the assignments updated by the event, effective-dated — module 1's machinery; the structures re-versioned where Chapter 4 moved bands); and <b>8 — the run</b> (the first affected run carrying the changes, its variance read pre-loaded with the cycle's grid as the expected-changes list — module 3's review consuming module 6's output: the machinery meshing).</p><p><b>The timing.</b> The cycle placed deliberately: aligned to the fiscal year and the appraisal season (the evidence fresh, the budget known), at the same time EVERY year (the workforce that knows review season builds expectations to it — the fixed payday's logic at annual scale: the cycle that drifts teaches that pay changes are weather), and with its effective date clean (the year's start or a fixed month — the mid-month effective date buying proration complexity for no benefit).</p><p><b>The cycle's size honesty.</b> The eleven-person reality, stated as every track states it: HealthTrade's cycle is the founder, the accountant, and the payroll seat, one afternoon of grid work and a week of letters — the SHAPE, small (the envelope a paragraph, the calibration a conversation, the approval a sitting) — and the discipline is not the paperwork's weight but the sequence's integrity: envelope before wishes, evidence before numbers, grid before letters, letters before slips. The firm that keeps the sequence at eleven people scales it to a hundred without redesign — which is the academy's standing promise, kept here at the layer where growing firms break most publicly (the fifty-person firm still doing rolling favours is the one whose pay chaos makes the trade's gossip).</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The increment matrix: evidence into naira", "13", "<p>The cycle's computational heart: the INCREMENT MATRIX — the published logic that converts a rating and a band position into a proposed increment. The matrix chapter.</p><p><b>The matrix's shape.</b> Two axes, one output: the RATING (the HR track's calibrated outcome — the honest scale of HR 5, with 'meets' as its honourable centre) crossed with the POSITION IN BAND (where the person's pay sits inside their grade's range — the compa-ratio concept taught practically: below the band's midpoint, at it, above it), yielding the increment percentage per cell — with the matrix's internal logic taught so consultants can design one, not just admire one: the HIGH-rating-LOW-position cell carries the largest increments (the strong performer paid under their band is the firm's biggest fairness debt and flight risk at once — the matrix pays the debt fastest), the MEETS-at-midpoint cell carries the maintenance increment (the real-pay-decay repair of Chapter 1 — the cost-of-living reality answered for the solid performer), the HIGH-position cells taper (the person at band top has outgrown the band — the answer is Chapter 5's promotion question or Chapter 4's band question, not an increment that breaches the band's own meaning), and the LOW-rating cells carry small-to-zero (with the capability process of HR 5 owning the person, not the matrix — the review is not the performance conversation; it consumes its output).</p><p><b>Why a matrix at all.</b> The argument, made once for every owner who calls it rigid: EVENNESS (same rating, same position, same increment — the favour system structurally displaced: the matrix cannot like anyone), BUDGET CONTROL (the matrix's cells priced against the population BEFORE approval — the envelope test run on the grid, the cells tuned until the total fits: the affordability engineered, not discovered), DEFENSIBILITY (the increment explained by pointing — the rating, the position, the cell: module 4's answered-by-pointing reaching the year's most sensitive question), and SPEED (the cycle's grid generated in minutes, reviewed in an afternoon — the rolling-negotiation year compressed into a sitting). The matrix's honest limits stated beside it: it PROPOSES — Chapter 2's stage 4 lets governance adjust with written reasons — and its calibration is only as good as the ratings feeding it (the leniency-inflated ratings of an uncalibrated appraisal season turn the matrix into an expensive rubber stamp: HR 5's calibration is this chapter's load-bearing dependency, and the two-track consultant sells them as the pair they are).</p><p><b>The below-band priority.</b> The matrix's first exception rule, promoted to policy: the person BELOW their band's floor is corrected FIRST, before the matrix runs — the correction to the band minimum is not an increment (not a reward, not a rating outcome); it is the structure enforcing its own promise (module 1's band architecture made a floor; the floor means something or nothing), funded ahead of the envelope's discretionary pool, and communicated as what it is (the correction letter distinct from the increment letter — the person told the band is real, which lands as the structure's justice exactly as HealthTrade's module 1 case landed). The chapter's closing frame: the matrix is the evenness law given a calculator — and the firm that publishes its logic (the axes and the philosophy, not the cell values' gossip) has told the workforce the one thing that defuses review season: the path to more money is evidence and growth, and the path is the same for everyone.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Band maintenance: the structure against the market", "13", "<p>Increments move people WITHIN bands; this chapter governs the bands THEMSELVES — the annual maintenance that keeps the structure honest against the market, inflation, and the law. The bands chapter.</p><p><b>The annual band review.</b> The structure's own review, run beside the cycle: each grade's band tested against three forces — the MARKET (what the trade pays for the role now: the labour-market read of HR 2, refreshed — the band that lags the market is the recruitment funnel's silent saboteur and the exit interview's recurring line; the read's sources practical: the firm's own hiring data (the offers declined and the asks that declined them), the trade's networks, the adviser's and peers' soundings), INFLATION (the real-value check of Chapter 1 applied to the band itself — the band unmoved for three years has drifted down the market by arithmetic alone), and the LAW (the national minimum wage as the absolute floor — the statutory minimum the adviser confirms current, against which the LOWEST band's floor is tested every year without exception: the minimum wage change is a compliance event, not a negotiation, and the reform era's movements make this an annual confirmation, not an assumption — the band floor below the legal floor is a violation wearing a structure).</p><p><b>The band move's machinery.</b> When the review moves a band: the structure RE-VERSIONED (module 1's amendment discipline — the new band effective-dated, the old preserved), the move's two distinct effects handled separately: the FLOOR effect (everyone below the new floor corrected to it — Chapter 3's below-band rule firing automatically at the band's own move) and the POSITION effect (everyone else's band position recalculated — the person at midpoint now below it, which feeds NEXT cycle's matrix rather than triggering immediate increments: the band move and the increment cycle are separate instruments, and conflating them double-spends the envelope), and the move COSTED before approval (the floor corrections' total is the band move's price tag — presented with the move, decided with open eyes).</p><p><b>Cost-of-living versus merit.</b> The distinction that keeps both honest, taught for the announcement as much as the arithmetic: the COLA-type adjustment (the across-the-board response to inflation — everyone's real pay maintained: applied as a band move plus a uniform component where the firm runs one, communicated as maintenance — <i>this is not a raise; this is your pay keeping its meaning</i>) versus the MERIT increment (the matrix's differentiated output — communicated as earned), because the firm that blends them into one number teaches that merit is automatic (the strong performer who got 12% learns 8% of it was inflation and feels the 4%), while the firm that names them separately gets credit for both — the maintenance honoured as fairness, the merit honoured as recognition: one letter, two lines, twice the trust.</p><p><b>The frozen-band autopsy.</b> The chapter's cautionary pattern, told for every prospect conversation: the firm whose bands froze for five years — real pay down by half, the market moved on, the recruitment funnel dried (the offers laughed at), the tenured staff trapped-then-gone in a wave (the exit interviews unanimous), and the panicked correction costing in one year what maintenance would have cost in five, plus the rebuilding — the arithmetic the consultant shows with the client's own inflation history, because the frozen band FEELS free (no decision, no cost, no letter) and is the most expensive pay decision a firm makes by not making it. The chapter's law-fragment: the structure is only as honest as its maintenance — bands reviewed annually, moved deliberately, floored legally, and separated cleanly from merit.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Promotion re-pricing & the equity reads", "13", "<p>The individual movements the cycle prices, and the population-level fairness reads that keep the whole structure honest. The equity chapter.</p><p><b>Promotion re-pricing.</b> HR 7's promotion event, receiving its pay discipline: the promoted person is priced INTO THE NEW BAND (the placement conversation — typically at or near the new band's floor-to-lower-third depending on readiness, per the firm's stated convention), NOT given a percentage on old pay — because the percent-on-old-pay promotion manufactures the trade's commonest inequity by arithmetic: the promoted-from-below person lands under the band (or under peers hired directly into the grade), doing the bigger job at the smaller pay, with the gap visible to everyone the moment module 4's band transparency exists (the promotion that nets a person less than an external hire for the same seat is the structure contradicting itself in public). The discipline's edge cases handled by rule: the promotion whose new-band placement exceeds the matrix-era increments is CORRECT (the promotion is a re-pricing event, not an increment — budgeted in Chapter 6's headcount plan, not the increment envelope), and the small-step promotion into an overlapping band placed thoughtfully (the overlap zone is where the placement convention earns its keep).</p><p><b>The compression read.</b> The first population scan, run annually beside the cycle: COMPRESSION — the gap between adjacent layers shrinking until it inverts (the new hire priced at today's market landing above the three-year incumbent whose increments lagged; the supervisor earning barely above the supervised — the differential that made the rung worth climbing, eroded): read by listing each grade's population against the one below (the overlaps flagged, the inversions treated as urgent — the incumbent who discovers the newcomer's premium is a resignation letter in draft, and module 4's confidentiality only delays the discovery), and repaired deliberately (the lagging incumbents' corrections prioritised in the envelope — the compression repair as a named budget line, because compression is the market's message that the internal maintenance fell behind the external reality, and the repair bill is the lag's invoice).</p><p><b>The equity scan.</b> The harder read, run with the care it demands: pay equity across the population — like work compared with like pay (within grade, controlling for band position and tenure: the scan asking whether any PATTERN — gender being the scan's classic and legally live axis, but tenure cohorts and hire-era effects equally — explains pay differences that evidence does not), read at altitude first (the grade-level averages and distributions — the HR track's numbers-are-questions epistemics governing absolutely: the pattern surfaced is a question for investigation, not a verdict for announcement), investigated by cases (the flagged pairs walked against their documents — the offer history, the increment trail, the red-circles: sometimes the difference has a documented story; sometimes it has none, and the none is the finding), and REPAIRED where found (the correction through the cycle's machinery, communicated per module 4's individual-change discipline — and the adviser engaged where the finding has legal weight, per the standing grammar). The scan's annual habit is the point: the firm that reads its own equity yearly repairs drifts of months; the firm that never reads repairs decades, publicly, under counsel.</p><p><b>The red-circle clock.</b> The legacy arrangements of module 1, managed over time: the red-circled pay (above-band, personal-to-holder) held FLAT through cycles (the band rises toward it — the increment matrix's high-position taper doing its designed work; the red circle is not punished, merely not compounded) until the band catches up and the circle dissolves (the arrangement re-absorbed into structure — the event noted, the history closed), with the discipline being patience-by-design: the red circle inherited from the pre-structure era is a debt the structure retires by growth, not by confrontation — the module's quietest lesson in how architecture fixes what arguments cannot.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The payroll budget: the owner's instrument", "13", "<p>The cycle's ceiling and the owner's steering wheel: the PAYROLL BUDGET — the layer where pay meets the business's arithmetic. The budget chapter.</p><p><b>Payroll cost in the business's shape.</b> The framing numbers, taught for the owner conversation: TOTAL payroll cost (module 1's third number, summed — gross plus employer statutory plus the variable layer's run-rate: the employer-cost model at population scale) read against the business (as a share of revenue and of gross margin — the trade's ratios varying by vertical: the distribution business and the pharmacy carry different payroll shapes, and the consultant benchmarks against the firm's own trend first, the vertical's norms second), TRENDED (the ratio's drift telling the structural story — payroll growing faster than gross margin is a trajectory conversation, not a crisis, when read early), and DECOMPOSED (the cost by branch and function via Accounts 6's dimensions — the payroll journal's cost-center addresses making the question 'where does our payroll go?' a report, not a research project: the trilogy's machinery answering the owner's biggest cost question from its ordinary operation).</p><p><b>The envelope.</b> The cycle's budget discipline, mechanised: the increment ENVELOPE set before the cycle (Chapter 2's stage one) from the business's numbers — the affordability modeled (the envelope's percentage against the year's plan: what the P&L carries, what the cash carries — the accountant's session, with Accounts 7's statements as the evidence), the envelope's COMPOSITION named (the maintenance share for the COLA reality, the merit pool for the matrix, the named repair lines — the below-band corrections, the compression repairs of Chapter 5 — each sized: the envelope with named compartments survives the cycle; the single number gets eaten by whoever moves first), and the envelope's honesty defended in both directions (the envelope too small for maintenance is Chapter 4's frozen-band autopsy on the instalment plan — the owner shown the real-pay arithmetic and the attrition bill before choosing it; the envelope inflated past the business's arithmetic is next year's retrenchment conversation prepaid — the sustainable raise beats the impressive one, and the workforce, told honestly, knows it).</p><p><b>The what-if machinery.</b> Module 1's modeling discipline at cycle scale: the review modeled before it is offered — the grid's scenarios costed (matrix tuning A versus B, the band move's price tag, the promotion slate's total — each scenario's monthly and annual cost, employer-statutory included, because the increment's true cost is the third number's delta, not the gross delta), the headcount plan integrated (HR 2's staffing frame — the planned hires' full cost in the same model: the year's people budget as ONE picture, increments and hires together, against one affordability), and the model's output being the owner's decision-grade page: this envelope, these bands, this slate — costing this, monthly, from these dates. The affordability conversation done this way is the module's quiet gift to the founder: the year's hardest numbers, decided once, with evidence, in daylight — instead of relitigated at every request, from memory, in corridors.</p><p><b>The budget's year.</b> The instrument's rhythm, closing the chapter: the budget set annually with the envelope, TRACKED monthly (the run's totals against the plan — module 3's approval already reads this; the budget line makes the reading cumulative), and VARIANCE-read quarterly (the drift explained: the overtime trend of module 5's read, the unplanned hires, the off-cycle creep — each a named cause, because the payroll budget that drifts silently is the business's largest cost unmanaged, and the one this trilogy exists to make boring).</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Governing the cycle: authorities, letters & the no-quiet-raises law", "13", "<p>The cycle's governance shell: who decides, how it is communicated, and the law that keeps every pay change inside the machinery. The governance chapter.</p><p><b>The authorities.</b> The cycle's separated powers, in the academy's pattern: the MATRIX and bands are configuration (the narrowest pen — changed at the cycle by the design conversation, versioned), the GRID is proposed by the machinery and adjusted by management within governance (the written-reason discipline for every deviation from the matrix's proposal — the adjustment without a reason is the favour system re-entering through the exception door), the APPROVAL is the named authority's in one sitting (the whole grid, every name — the founder at HealthTrade's scale; the committee where scale demands: module 3's real-approval standard at the year's biggest pay moment), and the EXECUTION is the payroll seat's through the standing machinery (the assignments by event, the letters from the grid — the deciders and the executors distinct, as every track requires). Nobody adjusts their own row: the cycle's participants with pay in the grid — the payroll seat, the managers — have their rows decided by their principals (HR 6's nobody-files-their-own-case law, at the pay table).</p><p><b>The letters.</b> The cycle's communication, per module 4's laws: INDIVIDUAL letters (the pay change is personal — the letter with the current, the new, the effective date, and the NET effect per module 1's modeling: the ₦20k gross that yields ₦14k net, shown, because the un-shown net difference is payday's guaranteed query), the maintenance and merit named separately (Chapter 4's two lines), the below-band and compression corrections named as corrections (the structure's justice, claimed), the letters BEFORE the first affected run with the week's runway (never-surprised-by-payday at the year's biggest change), and filed to the record (HR 7's event discipline — the letter is the assignment's authorising document). The population announcement beside them: the cycle's completion announced with its process honoured (the matrix's logic, the bands' review, the envelope's reality — the HOW public per module 4's transparency line, the WHOs private forever).</p><p><b>The no-quiet-raises law.</b> The cycle's perimeter, stated absolutely: every change to fixed pay travels through the CYCLE or a defined MOVEMENT EVENT — no third path (the quiet raise granted mid-year outside both is module 1's quiet edit wearing a kindness: invisible to the envelope, corrosive to the evenness, and discoverable by exactly the person it will wound most), with the mid-cycle EXCEPTION governed rather than denied: the genuine market correction (the critical role's band suddenly wrong — the band question handled as Chapter 4's machinery, off-cycle but on-process) and the counter-offer case (HR 6's honest framing applied: where the firm chooses to counter, the counter travels as a documented exception with its written reason, its approval, and its letter — rare by design, because Chapter's machinery makes the systematic version unnecessary: the firm whose bands are maintained and whose matrix pays evidence has removed the resignation-as-negotiation channel's fuel). The law's enforcement is structural, as always: the assignment writable only by its owning events (module 1's fence), the cycle and the movement events being the only events that own it — the quiet raise not forbidden but IMPOSSIBLE, which is this academy's preferred kind of rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's first review cycle", "16", "<p>The cycle, run end to end in its first year on structure. Every chapter clocks in.</p><p><b>The envelope and the evidence (Ch. 2, 6).</b> The sequence holds from the start: the accountant's affordability session lands the envelope in November (the year's numbers carrying a defined pool — compartments named: maintenance share, merit pool, and two repair lines pre-sized), the appraisal season concludes first (HR 5's calibrated ratings in — the calendar sequenced deliberately, and the one manager who tries to 'revisit' a rating during pay season is declined by the calendar itself: <i>ratings closed in October; we are pricing them, not rewriting them</i>). The band review runs beside it: the market read from the year's own hiring data (two declined offers at the counter-role band's floor — the market's message, received), the minimum-wage confirmation with the adviser (the lowest band's floor cleared with margin), and ONE band moved — the counter roles' band lifted, its floor corrections costed as the move's price tag and approved with open eyes.</p><p><b>The matrix and the grid (Ch. 3, 5).</b> The matrix designed in one sitting (three ratings by three band positions — nine cells, the philosophy walked: debt-paying cell largest, meets-at-midpoint as maintenance, high-position tapered), the grid generated in minutes — and the adjustments arrive as governance, not favours: TWO below-band corrections fire first (the band move's floor effect — the corrections funded from their named line, the letters drafted as corrections: the structure's justice, second edition), ONE compression flag (a three-year till operator overlapped by the year's new hire — the incumbent's repair prioritised with its written reason: <i>market moved; her increments lagged; the gap is ours, not hers</i>), and one promotion priced properly (the Ibadan senior storekeeper of HR 8's succession — placed into the new band's lower third, NOT percent-on-old: the placement exceeding any matrix cell, correctly, as a re-pricing event from the headcount plan's budget). The equity scan's first run reads clean at grade level with one flagged pair — walked against documents, the difference explained by a documented red-circle — the scan filed with its finding: <i>clean, one explained, repeat annually.</i></p><p><b>The letters and the run (Ch. 7, 2).</b> The founder approves the grid in one sitting — every name, every current-and-new, the envelope's total confirmed against the pool (<i>first year I've seen the whole picture on one page</i>). The letters go out with the week's runway: maintenance and merit as two lines (the branch reaction validating Chapter 4's design — the maintenance line landing as respect: <i>they named the inflation instead of pretending it was a raise</i>), net effects shown, the corrections named as corrections. The population announcement honours the process at the branch meetings. The first affected run's variance read is pre-loaded with the grid as its expected list — module 3's review consuming module 6's output, every variance matched, the run sealing clean on schedule.</p><p><b>The perimeter tested (Ch. 7).</b> The year delivers its test in month eight: a Lagos pharmacist resigns with a competitor's offer; the MD wants to counter. The machinery answers instead of the mood: the band checked first (current — the market read four months old and holding), the person's grid position reviewed (paid at band, rated honestly), and the honest conversation held per HR 6's framing — the firm counters ONCE, as a documented exception with its written reason and letter (the specialist licence's replacement cost pricing the exception), and the file notes the systemic answer beside the individual one: <i>if this recurs, it is a band question, not a counter-offer policy.</i> The pharmacist stays; the exception stays rare; the perimeter holds. The MD's cycle note closes the case: <i>pay used to be twenty-seven separate arguments spread across a year; now it is one season, one grid, one envelope — and the arguments stopped because the answers moved into the structure.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the sixth law", "13", "<p>The review layer's scar tissue. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the squeaky-wheel raise.</b> Symptom: raises to whoever asks loudest, whenever; the quiet diligent underpaid for their silence; resignation as the only reliable negotiation channel. Disease: rolling favours instead of a cycle (Ch. 1-2). Fix: one cycle on the calendar, the matrix pricing evidence, the perimeter law — the path to more money the same for everyone.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the frozen band.</b> Symptom: 'stable' salaries through years of inflation; offers laughed at; the tenured wave-exit; the panic correction costing five years' maintenance at once. Disease: real-pay decay unmanaged (Ch. 1, 4) — the most expensive decision made by not making it. Fix: the annual band review against market, inflation, and the minimum wage; maintenance named and funded; the decay arithmetic shown to the owner yearly.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the percent-on-old-pay promotion.</b> Symptom: the promoted earning under the band and under external peers; the bigger job at the smaller pay; the structure contradicting itself in public. Disease: the promotion treated as an increment instead of a re-pricing (Ch. 5). Fix: placement into the new band by convention, budgeted from the headcount plan — the rung worth climbing because the price honours it.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — compression ignored.</b> Symptom: new hires above incumbents; supervisors barely above supervised; the discovery followed by the resignation. Disease: the market repriced the entrance while the inside lagged (Ch. 5). Fix: the annual compression read, the inversions urgent, the repairs a named envelope line — the lag's invoice paid deliberately, not at exit interviews.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the unbudgeted cycle.</b> Symptom: increments decided then discovered unaffordable; the envelope negotiated after the wish-list; next year's retrenchment prepaying this year's generosity. Disease: capacity consulted last (Ch. 6). Fix: the envelope before the cycle, compartments named, scenarios costed at the third number — the sustainable raise beating the impressive one.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the quiet raise.</b> Symptom: pay changes outside any cycle or event; the grid that doesn't match the assignments; the favour discovered by exactly whom it wounds. Disease: the third path left open (Ch. 7). Fix: the assignment writable only by its owning events — the cycle and the movements — with the governed mid-cycle exception documented and rare: the quiet raise made impossible, not merely forbidden.</p><p><b>The sixth law.</b> The structure computes, the state rides, the run seals, the account renders, the one-offs join — and now the change itself is governed: <b>pay reviews run on structure and evidence — one cycle with its envelope set first, the matrix pricing calibrated evidence evenly, bands maintained against market, inflation, and law, promotions re-priced into their bands, equity read annually, every change lettered with its net, and no path to changed pay outside the machinery.</b> The firm that governs pay's changes as well as its computation has closed the layer's last leak — and module 7 now takes the machinery to its hardest single computation: the exit, where everything this track built must settle one person's whole account, once, correctly, on a deadline.</p>"),
]

